@model WordPicker.Web.Pages.WordLists.IndexModel
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Word Lists";
}

<div class="d-flex justify-content-between align-items-center mb-3">
    <h1>Word Lists</h1>
</div>

@if (!Model.WordLists.Any())
{
    <div class="alert alert-info">
        <p>No word lists yet. Create your first one below!</p>
    </div>
}
else
{
    <div class="list-group mb-4">
        @foreach (var list in Model.WordLists)
        {
            <div class="list-group-item d-flex justify-content-between align-items-center">
                <div>
                    <a asp-page="./Details" asp-route-id="@list.Id" class="text-decoration-none">
                        <strong>@list.Name</strong>
                    </a>
                    <br />
                    <small class="text-muted">Created: @list.CreatedAt.ToString("g")</small>
                </div>
                <a asp-page="./Delete" asp-route-id="@list.Id" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-danger">Delete</a>
            </div>
        }
    </div>
}

<hr class="my-4" />

<h2>Create New Word List</h2>

<div class="row">
    <div class="col-md-6">
        <form method="post">
            <div asp-validation-summary="ModelOnly" class="text-danger"></div>
            <div class="mb-3">
                <label asp-for="NewWordList.Name" class="form-label">Name</label>
                <input asp-for="NewWordList.Name" class="form-control" placeholder="Enter list name" />
                <span asp-validation-for="NewWordList.Name" class="text-danger"></span>
            </div>
            <button type="submit" class="btn btn-primary">Create List</button>
        </form>
    </div>
</div>

@section Scripts {
    <partial name="_ValidationScriptsPartial" />
}

parts.text:
Now I have a clear picture. Let me think about this and present the exploration to the user.

### 1. Promoting WordLists/Index to the main page

The current home page (`/Index`) is just a generic "Welcome" page. The user wants to make `/WordLists/Index` the root page instead and remove the old home page entirely.

Options:
- **Option A**: Move the WordLists/Index content to `/Pages/Index.cshtml` and delete the WordLists/Index page entirely. This loses the `/WordLists/Index` route.
- **Option B**: Keep WordLists/Index where it is, but change routing so `/` maps to it. In Razor Pages, this can be done via route templates on the page, or by configuring Razor Pages routing to point `/` to `/WordLists/Index`.
- **Option C**: Move the code from WordLists/Index to the root Index page, then delete WordLists/Index. This is the cleanest if they don't need `/WordLists/Index` as a separate route.

Actually, there's also the approach of adding `@page "/"` to the WordLists/Index page, but that would replace its existing route. Or we could use `AddPageRoute` in Program.cs.

Let me think about the cleanest approach:

**Option B with `@page` route**: We can't have two pages with route `/`. So if we keep WordLists/Index at its location, we'd need to either:
- Add `@page "/"` to WordLists/Index.cshtml — but then it still lives in the WordLists folder
- Or use conventions in Program.cs

I think the cleanest option is:
- Copy the WordLists/Index logic (both cshtml and cshtml.cs) to the root Index page
- Delete the old Index.cshtml and WordLists/Index completely
- Update any navigation links to point to `/` instead of `/WordLists/Index`

Wait, but that loses the cohesive folder organization. Let me think more...

Actually, the user said "promote the word list overview to be the main page." This naturally means the word list overview becomes the home page at route `/`. The question is whether the word list overview *also* remains accessible at `/WordLists/Index` or not.

Since they said the home page can be "completely removed," it seems like `/WordLists/Index` itself becomes the home page. The simplest approach would be to just update the route of the existing WordLists/Index page and update the navigation.

But there are multiple ways to do this:
1. Move the content to the root Index page (delete old Index, repurpose it)
2. Change the route on WordLists/Index to be `/` via `@page "/"` and then update navigation

For option 2, the WordLists/Index.cshtml would have `@page "/"` which overrides its route. That's the least disruptive to the folder organization. But then the folder structure doesn't match the route, which could be confusing.

Actually, in the .NET Razor Pages convention, the folder *is* the route by default. If we want to keep the file in the WordLists folder but have it serve as the root, we need route customization. This is fine.

Or, we could move the file. Let me just explore all this visually.

### 2. Sticky footer

Currently:
- `html { position: relative; min-height: 100%; }`
- `body { margin-bottom: 60px; }`
- The main content is wrapped in `<div class="container"><main role="main" class="pb-3">...</main></div>`
- The footer is `<footer class="border-top footer text-muted">`

This is the classic ASP.NET Core "sticky footer" approach — the body has a bottom margin equal to the footer height, and the footer is positioned absolutely at the bottom. But it's using `position: relative` on html and would need `position: absolute` on the footer with bottom: 0.

Wait, looking at the CSS more carefully:
```css
html {
  position: relative;
  min-height: 100%;
}
body {
  margin-bottom: 60px;
}
```

But there's no footer CSS rule for absolute positioning! The default Bootstrap template usually has something like:
```css
.footer {
  position: absolute;
  bottom: 0;
  width: 100%;
  white-space: nowrap;
  line-height: 60px;
}
```

That's missing! That's why the footer isn't consistently at the bottom. The `body { margin-bottom: 60px }` provides space for the footer, but the footer itself isn't positioned absolutely to sit in that space.

Actually wait, for Bootstrap 5 the default template structure in ASP.NET Core changed. Let me think about this differently.

The user says "the footer not consistently on the bottom" — meaning when the page content is short, the footer floats up rather than staying at the bottom of the viewport.

The modern approach using flexbox would be:
```css
html, body {
  height: 100%;
}
body {
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
}
.container {
  flex: 1 0 auto;
}
.footer {
  flex-shrink: 0;
}
```

Or we could use the `d-flex` and `min-vh-100` utility classes from Bootstrap directly in the layout.

Let me sketch out both ideas for the user and let them decide.

